# TCP Socket Operations
#
# Compatibility facade: `std.net.tcp` -> `std.nogc_sync_mut.io.tcp`.
#
# History / why this is a facade
# ------------------------------
# This module used to define its own `TcpListener` / `TcpStream` on top of the
# `tcp_listener_*` / `tcp_stream_*` externs in `net/sffi.spl`. NONE of those
# extern names is registered in `src/compiler_rust/common/src/runtime_symbols.rs`
# (the two apparent hits, `tcp_stream_connect` and `udp_socket_connect`, are
# entries in `compiler/src/security.rs`'s ambient-API POLICY table and in
# `compiler/src/effects.rs`'s NET_OPERATIONS effect table -- name lists, not
# implementations). An unregistered `@extern` returns nil SILENTLY under the
# JIT, so every method here failed with no error at all.
#
# The registered, working TCP implementation is `std.nogc_sync_mut.io.tcp`,
# which is fd-based over `rt_io_tcp_*` (`rt_io_tcp_bind`, `rt_io_tcp_connect`,
# `rt_io_tcp_read`, ... -- all present in `runtime_symbols.rs`). Re-exporting it
# reroutes every `use std.net.tcp.{...}` caller onto that live path.
#
# NOTE: `std.net.*` resolves to the `nogc_async_mut` tier FIRST from every tier
# (see `module_loader_resolve.spl`: "Default app mode is nogc_async_mut"), so
# this single file is what all four `net/tcp.spl` copies actually resolve to.
#
# API delta vs. the old dead implementation:
# - error type is `IoError` (not `SimpleError`); both expose `.message`, which
#   is all in-tree callers use.
# - sizes are `i64` (not `usize`).
# - `local_addr()` / `peer_addr()` return `Result<text, IoError>` (not `text?`).
# - `set_*` return `Result<(), IoError>` (not `bool`).
# - `set_nonblocking` is not on the live class; use
#   `std.nogc_sync_mut.io.tcp.tcp_backend_set_nonblocking(fd, enabled)`.

export use std.nogc_sync_mut.io.tcp.{TcpListener, TcpStream}
